signal.Reset resets the signal disposition for the entire process. When a
host application registers its own channel for SIGINT or SIGTERM,
signal.Reset removes that handler too. Removing signal.Reset avoids removing
the host handler, but handleShutdownSignal then does not re-raise the signal
after the flush. handleShutdownSignal consumes the signal and returns, the
same as on main.

Once sigCh is the last channel registered for a signal, signal.Stop(sigCh)
already restores the default disposition for that signal. handleShutdownSignal
does not re-raise the signal after it consumes the signal. A process with no
other signal handler then needs a second SIGINT or SIGTERM to terminate.

Re-raise the specific received signal instead of a process-wide reset. A
process with no other handler then terminates on the first signal, through
the OS default disposition. Because signal.Stop above deregisters sigCh
only, a host application with its own channel for that signal still
receives the signal.

```suggestion
	if err := Shutdown(ctx); err != nil {
		logger.Error("error flushing telemetry during shutdown", "error", err)
	} else {
		logger.Info("OpenTelemetry SDK shutdown completed successfully")
	}

	// Re-raise the received signal so a process with no other handler for it
	// terminates through the OS default disposition. Without this call, such a
	// process needs a second SIGINT or SIGTERM to terminate. Because the
	// re-raise deregisters no channel, a host application still receives the
	// signal on its own channel.
	if s, ok := sig.(syscall.Signal); ok {
		_ = syscall.Kill(os.Getpid(), s)
	}
}
```

If handleShutdownSignal re-raises the signal, these two doc comments must
change. Both comments currently state the opposite: that handleShutdownSignal
never exits or re-raises the signal.

```suggestion
// setupSignalHandler flushes the OTel SDK on SIGINT or SIGTERM. Buffered
// telemetry survives the shutdown. setupSignalHandler then re-raises the
// signal, so the process still terminates through its normal disposition,
// the OS default or a handler that the host application registers.
func setupSignalHandler() {
	registerSignalHandler.Do(func() {
		sigCh := make(chan os.Signal, 1)
		signal.Notify(sigCh, shutdownSignals()...)
		go handleShutdownSignal(sigCh)
	})
}

// handleShutdownSignal waits for the first signal on sigCh and flushes the
// SDK. handleShutdownSignal then re-raises the same signal, so the normal
// exit path for the process still runs, whether that path is the OS default
// or a handler that the host application registers.
